      <p style="margin-top:18px">Those labels are deliberately about fit, not a medal table. A product can be excellent at a job your club does not need.</p>

      <h2 id="different">The decision most comparisons miss: live queue or planned rounds?</h2>

      <p>Before comparing ratings, leaderboards or colour schemes, decide whether the software should plan the evening <strong>as it happens</strong> or <strong>as a sequence of rounds</strong>.</p>

      <p>A live system waits for a court to finish, looks at who is currently available and chooses the next game. It naturally absorbs late arrivals, early departures and courts finishing at different times.</p>

      <p>A round-based system generates a schedule: Round 1, Round 2, Round 3. This is wonderfully legible. Everyone can see the evening, rests can be distributed across the draw, and printing the sheet is possible. The cost is that reality keeps editing the plan.</p>

      <p>Neither is intrinsically superior. A stable nine-player group may prefer knowing all five games at 7pm. A thirty-player drop-in club with arrivals until 8:15 almost certainly benefits from live decisions.</p>

      <p>The second decision is equally important: <strong>what can outweigh queue order?</strong> Skill? Partner repeats? A requested rest? Some software treats queue fairness as a hard rule; some optimises a combined score. Ask the vendor to explain an example where the longest-waiting player is not selected.</p>

      <h2 id="epegboard">ePegboard: strongest when the picking policy itself matters</h2>

      <p><a href="/badminton-club-management-software">ePegboard</a> is built around the moment a court becomes free. Its picker considers expected closeness, skill spread, waiting fairness, queue order, partner/opponent variety, recent repeats, gender balance and avoiding straight-back-on games. Organisers can accept the suggestion, swap players, lock somebody in or build a game manually.</p>

      <p>The useful differentiator is not merely that it uses an algorithm; several competitors do. It exposes <em>why</em> a game was suggested and lets a club promote preferences to hard constraints - for example forcing the first player in the queue into the next pick. That matters for clubs where "fair" is a policy the room cares about, not just an optimisation score.</p>

      <p>Ratings and session history feed wider ePegboard workflows including handicap competitions and league-team support. Players do not need personal accounts for the club night; a shared kiosk can remain the centre of the session.</p>

      <p><strong>Choose it if:</strong> the hardest part of your night is making the next four fair, close and varied while keeping the logic understandable.</p>

      <p><strong>Look elsewhere if:</strong> your primary problem is integrated online booking/payment collection. That is currently more central to products such as ShuttleStats and broader club-management platforms.</p>

      <h2 id="shuttlsync">ShuttlSync: a strong browser-based recreational-club alternative</h2>

      <p><a href="https://www.shuttlsync.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ShuttlSync</a> is one of the closest current comparisons to ePegboard in philosophy. It is badminton-specific, browser-based and combines session sign-up, player shuffle, live scoring, ratings, club stats, seasons and achievements.</p>

      <p>Its shuffle uses games played, time sitting out and partner variety, and organisers can still move people manually. A particularly useful distinction is explicit <strong>offline session support</strong>, which is worth attention if your hall's Wi-Fi is unreliable.</p>

      <p>Its current free plan allows unlimited players and sessions with live play on up to two courts. Club Pro is listed at £9.99 per month and increases live sessions to four courts while adding rating insights.</p>

      <p><strong>Choose it if:</strong> you want a lightweight browser system with social/player features and offline operation, and your court count fits its current tier structure.</p>

      <p><strong>Check carefully if:</strong> you routinely run more than four courts; its current public pricing page describes live support up to four.</p>

      <h2 id="shuttlestats">ShuttleStats: strongest when money and attendance are part of the same problem</h2>

      <p><a href="https://shuttlestats.app/learn" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ShuttleStats</a> starts a little earlier in the club-night journey. Members can book sessions and pay through Stripe; capacity, waitlists, cancellation/refund handling and session credits sit alongside auto-matchmaking, a live court board, courtside scoring, ratings and player statistics.</p>

      <p>That makes it especially interesting for pay-as-you-go clubs where the organiser currently spends as much time chasing £12 session fees as choosing games.</p>

      <p>The business model is also different. Its public page currently says there is no subscription for the full club toolkit; instead it charges an agreed flat platform fee when a member makes a payment. Free sessions incur no platform fee.</p>

      <p><strong>Choose it if:</strong> booking, capacity and payment reconciliation are inseparable from running the night.</p>

      <p><strong>Check carefully if:</strong> you want ratings to follow a player consistently between different clubs. ShuttleStats states that each club's player data and profiles are isolated, so a person playing at two clubs has separate club-specific statistics.</p>

      <h2 id="nextfour">NextFour: the digital pegboard is the product</h2>

      <p><a href="https://nextfour.app/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NextFour</a> supports badminton alongside squash, tennis, padel and table tennis. Its language will feel immediately familiar to clubs replacing a physical board: players "peg on", watch the live queue and can use a courtside tablet in Terminal mode.</p>

      <p>Sessions can use Pegboard, Skill-based or Manual picking. This makes NextFour a good candidate for a club that wants a gradual migration rather than a philosophical change in how its night works.</p>

      <p>The pricing needs one extra check at decision time. NextFour says core club nights are free, while paid plans unlock features including Pegboard, Skill-based picking and Terminal mode; subscriptions are handled through the mobile app stores. The public support page does not currently put one simple web price beside those tiers.</p>

      <p><strong>Choose it if:</strong> a familiar, mobile-first pegboard experience matters more than building a large badminton-specific data ecosystem around the club.</p>

      <h2 id="qourtx">QourtX: broader racquet-club operations with a strong wallboard</h2>

      <p><a href="https://www.qourtx.com/badminton-club-management" target="_blank" rel="noopener">QourtX</a> is aimed across racquet sports rather than only badminton. Its badminton offering includes a smart waiting queue, skill-based court assignments, skip tracking, configurable rest, guest check-in, session analytics and a live wallboard intended for a large display.</p>

      <p>That broader scope can be an advantage for a multi-sport venue or a club wanting membership and operations around the session rather than only game rotation.</p>

      <p>There is one thing we would verify before publishing a committee budget. QourtX's current public pages are not perfectly consistent about commercial packaging: its badminton page says free for clubs up to 50 members with larger clubs starting at £5.99/month, while its main site describes a free Local tier and a substantially different per-active-member Club tier. That may reflect a product or pricing transition, but it is enough reason to ask for the applicable plan in writing.</p>

      <p><strong>Choose it if:</strong> you want the club-night wallboard inside a broader racquet-club management product.</p>

      <h2 id="sportrevive">SportRevive: best thought of as a very smart fixture sheet</h2>

      <p><a href="https://sportrevive.com/badminton-fixture-generator" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SportRevive</a> takes a different route. Tick who arrived, choose courts and desired games per player, and it generates a full set of rounds with teams balanced by rating, partners rotated and sit-outs shared.</p>

      <p>That is attractive because the result is inspectable before anybody hits a shuttle. It can be printed, run from phones and edited when someone arrives late or leaves. Scores feed the ratings and recap afterwards.</p>

      <p>The trade-off is inherent in the model: it begins from a planned sheet rather than independent courts releasing at unpredictable times. Some clubs will prefer that structure; some will find live next-game selection more natural.</p>

      <p><strong>Choose it if:</strong> your attendance is reasonably stable and you like the clarity of knowing the rounds in advance.</p>

      <h2 id="test">How to test any of them properly</h2>

      <p>Do not test with eight perfectly behaved imaginary players. That is the software equivalent of buying hiking boots by standing on carpet.</p>

      <p>Run a trial that includes:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>a late arrival after several games have started;</li>
        <li>one player asking to sit out and then rejoin;</li>
        <li>two courts finishing within seconds of each other;</li>
        <li>a pairing you deliberately want to keep together;</li>
        <li>an obviously bad algorithm suggestion that you override;</li>
        <li>a player leaving before the end;</li>
        <li>a wrong score that needs correcting;</li>
        <li>and, if your hall is unreliable, loss of internet.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>Then ask the organiser one question: <strong>did this remove decisions from my head, or merely move them onto a screen?</strong></p>

      <p>Also test exit. Export whatever data the product says belongs to your club. A system can be free to enter and expensive to leave if years of results cannot come with you.</p>

